    <h1 class="article-title">Power BI Consulting for Small Business: Dashboards That Drive Faster Decisions</h1>
    <p class="article-dek">Most small businesses don't have a data problem — they have a "where is the data" problem. Here's how a proper Power BI setup replaces five logins and a Monday morning of guesswork with one dashboard you can actually trust.</p>

    <div class="article-byline">
      <div class="article-avatar" style="background:#2E3A55;">LI</div>
      <div><div class="article-author">Lindsay</div><div class="article-role">Consultant, Tycoon Clap</div></div>
      <div class="article-time">8 min read</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-hero-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 160" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="28" y="20" width="184" height="120" rx="14" fill="#DCEFE9"/><rect x="44" y="36" width="152" height="12" rx="6" fill="#fff"/><circle cx="54" cy="42" r="3" fill="#E9584C"/><circle cx="64" cy="42" r="3" fill="#F3B94D"/><circle cx="74" cy="42" r="3" fill="#357566"/><rect x="44" y="58" width="44" height="26" rx="6" fill="#357566"/><rect x="98" y="58" width="44" height="26" rx="6" fill="#E9584C"/><rect x="152" y="58" width="44" height="26" rx="6" fill="#F3B94D"/><rect x="44" y="94" width="92" height="36" rx="6" fill="#fff"/><rect x="54" y="112" width="10" height="12" rx="2" fill="#4C9C8B"/><rect x="70" y="104" width="10" height="20" rx="2" fill="#357566"/><rect x="86" y="108" width="10" height="16" rx="2" fill="#2E3A55"/><rect x="102" y="100" width="10" height="24" rx="2" fill="#E9584C"/><rect x="146" y="94" width="50" height="36" rx="6" fill="#2E3A55"/><path d="M152 120l12-10 10 6 16-14" stroke="#F3B94D" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></div>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <article class="article-content" id="articleContent">
        <p>You don't need a 1,500-deployment enterprise firm to get real value out of Power BI. Most small businesses, e-commerce brands, and SaaS teams need something much simpler: their sales, ad spend, and operations numbers in one place, updating on their own, without a full-time analyst to babysit it.</p>

        <h2 id="decide-faster">How Power BI helps small businesses decide faster</h2>
<p>A founder checking five different logins before a Monday meeting isn't making a decision — they're doing detective work. Power BI replaces that with a single view: sales, ad spend, and operations updating in real time, so a decision that used to take a week of pulling reports now takes minutes. That speed is the actual return on investment, more than the dashboard itself.</p>
        <div class="stat-callout"><div class="stat-callout-icon"><svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#4C9C8B" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 20V10M18 20V4M6 20v-4"/></svg></div><p>Teams that move from spreadsheet reporting to a proper Power BI setup usually cut their weekly reporting time by more than half within the first month.</p></div>
<h2 id="ecommerce-saas">Power BI for e-commerce and SaaS reporting</h2>
<p>For an e-commerce brand, that single dashboard usually means ad spend, conversion rate, and inventory sitting next to each other — so a founder can see a stockout coming before it costs a week of lost sales, instead of finding out from a customer email. For a SaaS team, it's MRR, churn, and support ticket volume in one place, so a spike in cancellations gets caught the same week it starts, not at the next board meeting.</p>
        <div class="article-pull">"A decision that used to take a week of pulling reports now takes minutes."</div>
        <p>This is where a lot of "Power BI consulting services" pitches miss the mark for smaller companies: they're built for enterprises with dedicated data teams, not for a 10-person business that needs three dashboards done right and left alone to run.</p>
<h2 id="excel-vs-power-bi">Excel vs. Power BI for small business — when do you actually need to switch?</h2>
<p>Excel isn't wrong for a small business. It's wrong once you've outgrown it — and that point is easier to spot than most founders think.</p>
        <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
          <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Excel is still fine</th><th>Time to move to Power BI</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Data sources</td><td>Everything fits in one or two spreadsheets</td><td>Data lives in 3+ places (CRM, ads, Shopify, Stripe)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Update frequency</td><td>Weekly manual refresh is fine</td><td>You need it live, or you're wasting hours copy-pasting</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who reads it</td><td>Just you</td><td>A team or investors need self-serve access</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Formula complexity</td><td>Simple sums and lookups</td><td>You're building anything resembling a "model"</td></tr>
        </table></div>
        <p>If two or more of the right-hand column sound familiar, that's usually the actual trigger — not company size or revenue, which is what most agencies use as their pitch instead.</p>
<h2 id="common-mistakes">Where small businesses get Power BI wrong</h2>
<p>The most common mistake is treating a Power BI project as a one-time export job — build a report, forget it, watch it go stale. It only earns its keep when it's connected live to the source systems, refreshing on a schedule, and reviewed monthly so the metrics tracked still match what the business actually cares about three months later.</p>
<h2 id="cost">How much does Power BI consulting cost for a small business?</h2>
<p>Industry-wide, consulting rates for Power BI work typically run $50–$200/hour depending on experience and location — but for a small business, the more useful number is the total project cost for a focused build, not an hourly rate meant for enterprise-scale engagements. A tightly scoped project — three to five core dashboards, connected to your actual tools, with training for your team — is a fundamentally different (and far cheaper) engagement than a multi-department enterprise rollout, and should be priced that way.</p>
<h2 id="setup">What a small-business Power BI setup actually involves</h2>
<p>You don't need a "deployment pipeline" with separate dev, test, and production environments to get value — that's enterprise-scale process for enterprise-scale risk. What you do need:</p>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#4C9C8B" stroke-width="2.5"><path d="M4 12l5 5L20 6"/></svg><span>Your real data sources connected directly — CRM, ad platforms, Shopify/Stripe, not manual exports</span></li>
          <li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#4C9C8B" stroke-width="2.5"><path d="M4 12l5 5L20 6"/></svg><span>A handful of dashboards built around the decisions you actually make weekly, not every metric available</span></li>
          <li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#4C9C8B" stroke-width="2.5"><path d="M4 12l5 5L20 6"/></svg><span>A short handoff session so your team can actually use it — not a black box only the consultant understands</span></li>
          <li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#4C9C8B" stroke-width="2.5"><path d="M4 12l5 5L20 6"/></svg><span>Power BI Desktop is free to build in — for most small businesses, the real cost is the setup work, not the license</span></li>
        </ul>
<h2 id="power-platform">Growing into the Power Platform</h2>
<p>Power BI is one piece of a larger Power Platform — Power Apps for custom internal tools, Power Automate for workflows, and Power BI for reporting, all sharing the same data layer. You don't need this on day one, but building your first dashboards with this in mind means you're not rebuilding your reporting stack from scratch the moment you add a second tool.</p>
<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>How long does a small-business Power BI setup take?</summary><p class="faq-a">A focused build of three to five dashboards connected to your core tools typically takes a few weeks, depending on how clean your data sources are and how many systems need to be connected.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do I need a data team to use Power BI?</summary><p class="faq-a">No. Once dashboards are built and connected, they refresh on a schedule and anyone on your team can read them. A short handoff session covers filtering, drilling down, and what each metric means.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is Power BI free?</summary><p class="faq-a">Power BI Desktop is free to build reports in. Sharing dashboards with a team through the Power BI service requires paid licenses, but for most small businesses the main cost is the setup work, not the license.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Should I keep using Excel after moving to Power BI?</summary><p class="faq-a">Often yes — Excel stays useful for quick ad-hoc analysis and can even be a data source. Power BI takes over the recurring reporting that eats your time every week.</p></details>
        </div>
